**Identity merges (guesses):**
- **Alan Wang:** The table above merges <email redacted> (121 lines) and <email redacted> (1 line). Unmerged, the 163.com address alone has 121 lines (4.0%). The existing `.mailmap` already folds in `Alanscut <<email redacted>>` (90 lines). The same address also appears under the name Alan_scut.
- **tomasi:** I merged `<email redacted>` (61 lines) and `tibarra@corp.example` (55 lines) to get 116. Unmerged, that is 61 lines (2.0%) and 55 lines (1.8%), listed as tomasi and Tomás Ibarra. The merge rests only on names and addresses. Until you confirm it, treat the combined figure as a guess.
- **Same address, several names:** PeterAlfredLee also appears as Lee, and Stephan also appears as Stephan Gatzka (3 lines). The tool counts each of these as one person, and it printed one more such case that I did not list.
- I did not write anything to `.mailmap`.

**Left out:**
- **Reformat commit:** `413c6d1` (Priya Nair, "run clang-format over cJSON.c and cJSON.h") is listed in `.git-blame-ignore-revs`, so its lines go back to the earlier authors. It rewrote 2,303 lines across both files, so it would otherwise have taken ownership of most of the code.
- **Bot:** github-actions[bot] is excluded (5 lines, 1 commit) and is not credited.
- **Mechanical rewrites:** The tool checked 307 commits holding surviving lines and set none aside. It flagged 58 commits that look mechanical, mostly Max's "reformatting: …" commits. I checked `f831aef`. It added 28 lines and removed 1, so it is a real code change and I kept it. I did not inspect the others.
- **Copy detection:** The figures use `-C`, with no deep copy detection. The tool reported no shift of more than 2 points for any author.

**Limits:** Surviving lines say nothing about quality, review work or design. Code that was deleted on purpose counts for nobody. They show whose code is still here, not who is the best engineer.

